{
  "dom_id": "step-q105202",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14105016/assets/8f4d72bd431d4f06daa3ea2be20d0ebb21fd736b944be77331c72c2e47e3144f.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[0,\\infty ]\\to (-\\infty ,5]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(0)=f(2)$\n- $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(0)=5$ and $f(2)<5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[0,\\infty )$ that map to the same element in $(-\\infty ,5].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[0,\\infty ),$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
